=== lesson
id: py-string-methods
title: String methods and f-strings
summary: Strings come with built-in tools called **methods** for changing case, trimming spaces, finding and replacing text, and splitting text apart. Then **f-strings** let you drop values straight into text, rounded and lined up the way you want.

=== learn
### Methods: tools that belong to a value
A **method** is a function that belongs to a value. You call it with a **dot** after the value, then the method's name and round brackets:
```python
print("hello".upper())
```
```output
HELLO
```
Read `"hello".upper()` as: take the string `"hello"` and run its `upper` method. Methods work on variables too: if `name = "ada"`, then `name.upper()` is `"ADA"`.

> The brackets are what make the method run. `name.upper` without brackets doesn't call it: it's just the method itself, and printing it shows something like `<built-in method upper of str object at 0x...>` instead of the text you wanted.

### Changing case
| Method | What it does | `"ada LOVELACE"` becomes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `.upper()` | every letter to capitals | `"ADA LOVELACE"` |
| `.lower()` | every letter to small letters | `"ada lovelace"` |
| `.title()` | first letter of each word capital, the rest small | `"Ada Lovelace"` |

Characters that aren't letters (digits, spaces, symbols) are left alone.

### Methods give back a new string
Strings can't be changed (they're immutable, from the last lesson), so a method can't change the string it's called on. Instead it **returns** a new string:
```python
name = "ada"
name.upper()
print(name)
shout = name.upper()
print(shout)
```
```output
ada
ADA
```
Line 2 makes `"ADA"` but doesn't store it anywhere, so it's thrown away, and `name` is still `"ada"`. To keep the result, store it: `shout = name.upper()`, or replace the old value with `name = name.upper()`.

### strip(): trim spaces from the ends
Text typed by people often has extra spaces at the start or end. `.strip()` removes them (and tabs and line breaks) from **both ends**, but never from the middle:
```python
raw = "   ada lovelace  "
print("[" + raw + "]")
print("[" + raw.strip() + "]")
```
```output
[   ada lovelace  ]
[ada lovelace]
```
The square brackets are only there to make the spaces visible. `.lstrip()` trims only the left end, and `.rstrip()` only the right end.

### replace(): swap one piece of text for another
`.replace(old, new)` gives a new string with **every** copy of `old` replaced by `new`:
```python
s = "I like cats. cats are great."
print(s.replace("cats", "dogs"))
print(s.replace(" ", ""))
```
```output
I like dogs. dogs are great.
Ilikecats.catsaregreat.
```
Replacing with `""` (the empty string) deletes the text. Like everything with strings, `replace` is case-sensitive: `"Cats"` would not match `"cats"`.

### Searching: find, count, startswith, endswith
| Method | Gives back | Example with `s = "banana"` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `s.find(text)` | the index where `text` first appears, or `-1` if it's not there | `s.find("na")` is `2`; `s.find("x")` is `-1` |
| `s.count(text)` | how many times `text` appears (without overlapping) | `s.count("a")` is `3` |
| `s.startswith(text)` | `True` or `False` | `s.startswith("ban")` is `True` |
| `s.endswith(text)` | `True` or `False` | `s.endswith("a")` is `True` |

`find` returns an index, so you can use it with slicing: in `"ada@example.com"`, `find("@")` is `3`, and everything before the `@` is `email[:email.find("@")]`.

> `find` gives `-1` when the text isn't there, and `-1` is also a valid index (the last character). Always check for `-1` before slicing with the result.

### isdigit(): is it all digits?
`.isdigit()` is `True` when the string has at least one character and **every** character is a digit:

| Expression | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `"2026".isdigit()` | `True` |
| `"20 26".isdigit()` | `False` (the space isn't a digit) |
| `"-5".isdigit()` | `False` (the minus sign isn't a digit) |
| `"".isdigit()` | `False` (no characters at all) |

It's useful before `int()`: `int("abc")` would be a ValueError, and `"abc".isdigit()` tells you that in advance. (`.isalpha()` does the same check for letters.)

### split() and join(): taking text apart and putting it back
`.split()` breaks a string into pieces. With no argument it splits wherever there are spaces; with an argument it splits at that text:
```python
words = "the quick brown fox".split()
print(words)
print(words[0])
parts = "2026-10-05".split("-")
print(parts)
```
```output
['the', 'quick', 'brown', 'fox']
the
['2026', '10', '05']
```
The result is a **list**: several values in square brackets, separated by commas. Lists get their own lessons later; for now, you can index a list just like a string: `words[0]` is the first piece.

`.join()` does the opposite: it glues the pieces of a list together, putting the string it's called on **between** them:
```python
words = "the quick brown fox".split()
print("-".join(words))
print("".join(words))
```
```output
the-quick-brown-fox
thequickbrownfox
```
Notice the order: the glue comes first, `"-".join(words)`, not `words.join("-")`.

### Chaining methods
Each method returns a new string, so you can call another method on the result straight away. Python works left to right:
```python
raw = "  Hello World  "
print(raw.strip().lower().replace(" ", "_"))
```
```output
hello_world
```
Step by step: `strip()` gives `"Hello World"`, `lower()` gives `"hello world"`, and `replace(" ", "_")` gives `"hello_world"`.

### f-strings: values inside text
Joining text with `+` and `str()` gets clumsy. An **f-string** is a string with an `f` right before the opening quote. Inside it, anything in curly braces `{ }` is worked out and dropped into the text:
```python
name = "Ada"
age = 36
print(f"{name} is {age} years old")
print(f"Next year she will be {age + 1}")
```
```output
Ada is 36 years old
Next year she will be 37
```
No `str()` is needed: f-strings convert values to text for you. Any expression works inside the braces, even `{name.upper()}`.

> Forget the `f` and the braces are printed as they are: `"{name} is {age}"` prints exactly `{name} is {age}`.

### Formatting numbers in f-strings
After the expression, a colon `:` adds a **format**:

| Code | Output | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `f"{3.14159:.2f}"` | `3.14` | round to 2 decimal places |
| `f"{7:.2f}"` | `7.00` | always show 2 decimals, good for money |
| `f"[{42:>5}]"` | `[   42]` | right-align in 5 characters |
| `f"[{42:<5}]"` | `[42   ]` | left-align in 5 characters |
| `f"[{'ab':>5}]"` | `[   ab]` | text can be aligned too |

`.2f` means "a float with 2 digits after the point". Padding to a fixed width is how you line values up in columns:
```python
print(f"{'tea':<8}{2.5:>6.2f}")
print(f"{'coffee':<8}{13:>6.2f}")
```
```output
tea       2.50
coffee   13.00
```
Each name gets 8 characters, and each price is right-aligned in 6 characters with 2 decimals, so the decimal points line up.

=== keypoints
- A **method** is called with a dot and brackets: `name.upper()`. Without the brackets it doesn't run.
- String methods **return a new string** and never change the original. Store the result: `name = name.strip()`.
- `.upper()`, `.lower()`, `.title()` change case; `.strip()` trims spaces from both ends; `.replace(old, new)` swaps every match.
- `.find(t)` gives the first index of `t` or `-1`; `.count(t)` counts; `.startswith(t)`, `.endswith(t)` and `.isdigit()` give `True`/`False`.
- `.split()` breaks text into a list of pieces; `"-".join(pieces)` glues them back with `-` between.
- `f"{name} is {age}"` drops values into text. `{x:.2f}` rounds to 2 decimals; `{x:>5}` and `{x:<5}` align in a width of 5.

=== example: Cleaning up a name
intro: One messy string, several methods. Notice that `raw` itself never changes.
```python
raw = "   ada LOVELACE  "
clean = raw.strip()
print(clean)
print(clean.title())
print(clean.upper())
print(clean.lower())
print(len(raw), len(clean))
print(clean.find("LOVE"), clean.count("A"))
```
1: The text has 3 spaces at the start, 2 at the end, and mixed capitals.
2: strip() returns a new string without the outer spaces. The space between the two names stays.
3: Prints ada LOVELACE: the spaces are gone, the letters are untouched.
4: title() capitalizes the first letter of each word and lowers the rest.
5-6: upper() and lower() change every letter.
7: raw still has all 17 characters, because strip() made a new string instead of changing raw. clean has 12.
8: find gives the index where LOVE starts (4, after "ada "), and count finds only one capital A: the word ada is in small letters.

=== example: A receipt with f-strings
intro: f-strings drop values into text, round prices and line up columns.
```python
item = "coffee"
price = 3.5
qty = 2
total = price * qty
print(f"{qty} x {item} = {total}")
print(f"Total: ${total:.2f}")
print(f"[{item:>10}]")
print(f"[{item:<10}]")
print(f"{item.upper()} costs {price:.2f} each")
```
4: total is 3.5 * 2, which is the float 7.0.
5: Each {...} is replaced by the variable's value. No str() needed.
6: :.2f shows exactly 2 decimals, so 7.0 prints as 7.00. The $ is just a character in the text.
7: :>10 right-aligns coffee in a space 10 characters wide: 4 spaces, then the 6 letters.
8: :<10 left-aligns it: the 6 letters, then 4 spaces.
9: Any expression works inside the braces, even a method call.

=== animation: Methods return new strings
view: vars
intro: Watch the table: each method makes a new value in a new variable, and `name` never changes.
```python
name = "  grace hopper "
trimmed = name.strip()
title = trimmed.title()
shout = title.upper()
count = shout.count("R")
where = shout.find("HOPPER")
print(name)
print(shout, count, where)
```
1: name holds the text with 2 spaces at the start and 1 at the end.
2: strip() returns {name.strip()!r}. name itself is not changed: watch it stay the same.
3: title() capitalizes each word of trimmed: {trimmed.title()!r}.
4: upper() makes every letter a capital: {title.upper()!r}.
5: count("R") counts the capital Rs in {shout!r}: there are {shout.count("R")}.
6: find("HOPPER") gives the index where HOPPER starts: {shout.find("HOPPER")}, because "GRACE " takes up indexes 0 to 5.
7: name still has its spaces, because no method changed it.
8: Prints {shout} {count} {where}

=== animation: Building f-strings
view: vars
intro: Each f-string is worked out once, when its line runs, and the result is ordinary text.
```python
name = "Ada"
score = 92.456
rank = 1
line = f"{rank}. {name} scored {score:.1f}"
print(line)
padded = f"{name:<6}|{score:>8.2f}|"
print(padded)
```
4: Python fills in the f-string's three pairs of braces: rank is {rank}, name is {name!r}, and score rounded to 1 decimal is {round(score, 1)}.
5: line is ordinary text now: {line!r}
6: :<6 pads Ada with spaces to 6 characters; :>8.2f rounds the score to {round(score, 2)} and right-aligns it in 8 characters.
7: The | marks show the widths: {padded}

=== task: mcq easy
title: What does upper() give?
prompt: What does this print?
code:
```python
print("hello".upper())
```
check: output
* `HELLO` :: Right. `.upper()` returns a new string with every letter in capitals.
- `Hello` :: That's what `.title()` (or `.capitalize()`) would give. `.upper()` capitalizes every letter.
- `hello` :: The method's result is printed, and `.upper()` changes every letter to a capital.
- An error :: Methods can be called directly on a string value like `"hello"`, not only on variables.
hint: The method's name says what it does.
hint: It works on every letter, not just the first.
right: `.upper()` builds a new string with every letter in capitals and returns it, and `print` shows that new string.
wrong: `.upper()` turns every letter into a capital letter: `"hello".upper()` is `"HELLO"`. Calling methods directly on a string value works fine.

=== task: predict easy
title: Does the original change?
code:
```python
word = "Banana"
print(word.lower())
print(word.count("a"))
print(word)
```
hint: `.lower()` returns a new string. Is `word` itself changed?
hint: `.count("a")` counts small `a`s only. Capital `B` isn't an `a` anyway.
right: `word.lower()` returns `banana` but doesn't change `word`. `"Banana"` has three small `a`s, so `count` gives `3`. The last print shows `word` unchanged: `Banana`.
wrong: Methods return new strings and leave the original alone. So line 2 prints `banana`, line 3 counts the small `a`s (three), and line 4 prints the original `Banana`.
wrong[banana\n3\nbanana]: `word.lower()` returned a new string, but it was only printed, never stored. `word` still holds `Banana` with its capital B.
wrong[banana\n2\nBanana]: Count the `a`s in `Banana`: B-**a**-n-**a**-n-**a**. There are three.
wrong[Banana\n3\nBanana]: Line 2 prints the result of `.lower()`, which is all small letters: `banana`.

=== task: fill easy
title: Trim the spaces
prompt: Fill the blank with a method name so that the program prints:
```output
[Ada]
```
code:
```python
name = "   Ada  "
clean = name.??()
print("[" + clean + "]")
```
answer: strip
hint: You need to remove the spaces from both ends.
hint: Its name sounds like taking something off.
right: `.strip()` removes spaces from both ends of the string, and returns the trimmed text, which is stored in `clean`.
wrong: `.strip()` removes the spaces at the start and end: `"   Ada  ".strip()` is `"Ada"`.
wrong[lstrip]: `.lstrip()` only trims the left end, so the 2 spaces at the end would stay: `[Ada  ]`. Use `strip`.
wrong[rstrip]: `.rstrip()` only trims the right end, so the 3 spaces at the start would stay. Use `strip`.
wrong[replace]: `.replace` needs two arguments, the old text and the new text. To trim the ends, `strip` is the tool.

=== task: predict easy
title: Three kinds of case
code:
```python
s = "hello WORLD"
print(s.upper())
print(s.lower())
print(s.title())
```
wrong[HELLO WORLD\nhello world\nHello WORLD]: `.title()` makes the first letter of each word a capital **and the rest of the word small**, so WORLD becomes World.
wrong[HELLO WORLD\nhello world\nHello world]: `.title()` capitalizes the first letter of **every** word, not only the first one: Hello World.
wrong[HELLO WORLD\nhello WORLD\nHello World]: `.lower()` changes every letter to a small letter, including the capitals in WORLD.
hint: upper means all capitals, and lower means all small letters.
hint: title means each word starts with a capital, and the rest of each word is small.
right: `.upper()` makes every letter a capital and `.lower()` makes every letter small. `.title()` capitalizes the first letter of each word and makes the rest small, so even the capitals in WORLD become `World`.
wrong: Apply each method to the whole of "hello WORLD": upper → HELLO WORLD, lower → hello world, title → Hello World (each word starts with a capital, and the rest of each word is small).

=== task: code easy
title: Loud and quiet
prompt: `word` is already set, and the checks try several words. Create `loud`, the word in capital letters, and `quiet`, the word in small letters. For `"Hello"`, `loud` is `"HELLO"` and `quiet` is `"hello"`.
starter:
```python
# word is already set before your code runs
# your code here
```
solution:
```python
loud = word.upper()
quiet = word.lower()
```
tests:
- loud
- quiet
cases:
- word = "Hello"
- word = "PyThOn"
- word = "abc"
- word = "123go"
fail[1]: For "Hello", `loud` should be 'HELLO' and `quiet` should be 'hello'. If one of them is still 'Hello', the method's result wasn't stored: write `loud = word.upper()`, so the new string is kept.
error[NameError]: Spell `word`, `loud` and `quiet` exactly like this.
error[AttributeError]: Check the method names: `.upper()` and `.lower()`, with a dot in front and brackets after.
require[.upper()]: Use `.upper()` for the capitals.
require[.lower()]: Use `.lower()` for the small letters.
hint: The two methods you need are named after what they do.
hint: Store each method's result in its own variable.
hint: `loud = word.upper()` and `quiet = word.lower()`.
right: `.upper()` and `.lower()` each return a new string, and storing them in `loud` and `quiet` keeps them; `word` itself never changes. Characters that aren't letters stay as they are, so "123go" becomes "123GO" and "123go".
wrong: Call a method on `word` and store what it returns: `loud = word.upper()` and `quiet = word.lower()`. The method makes a new string, and only storing it keeps it.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Forgot the brackets
prompt: What does this program print?
code:
```python
name = "ada"
print(name.upper)
```
- `ADA` :: It would, with brackets: `name.upper()`. Without them, the method never runs.
- `ada` :: print doesn't show the string in `name` here. It shows the method itself.
* Something like `<built-in method upper of str object at 0x...>` :: Right. Without the round brackets, `name.upper` is the method itself, so print shows a description of it instead of running it.
- An error :: Writing a method's name without brackets is allowed; it just doesn't call the method. The program runs, but it prints something you didn't want.
hint: What makes a method run?
hint: Look for round brackets after `upper`.
right: The brackets are what call a method. `name.upper` without them is the method itself, so print shows a description like `<built-in method upper of str object at 0x...>`. Write `name.upper()` to get `ADA`.
wrong: A method only runs when you put round brackets after its name. Without them, print shows the method itself, something like `<built-in method upper of str object at 0x...>`. With the brackets, `name.upper()` gives `ADA`.

=== task: fill easy
title: Cut the date into pieces
prompt: Fill the blank with a method name so that the program cuts the date at each dash and prints the year:
```output
2026
```
code:
```python
date = "2026-10-05"
parts = date.??("-")
print(parts[0])
```
answer: split
wrong[strip]: `.strip("-")` only removes dashes from the two ends, and there are none there. So `parts` is still the whole date, and `parts[0]` is its first character: 2. `.split("-")` cuts the text at every dash.
wrong[replace]: `.replace` needs two arguments, the old text and the new text, so this is a TypeError. To cut text into pieces, use `.split`.
wrong[find]: `.find("-")` gives a number, the index of the first dash (4), and you can't take `[0]` of a number. `.split("-")` gives the pieces.
hint: You need the method that breaks text into pieces.
hint: It gives a list, and `parts[0]` is the first piece.
right: `date.split("-")` cuts the text at every dash and gives the list ['2026', '10', '05']. `parts[0]` is the first piece: the year.
wrong: `.split("-")` breaks the date into pieces wherever there's a dash: ['2026', '10', '05']. Then `parts[0]` is "2026".

=== task: predict easy
title: Count and find
code:
```python
river = "mississippi"
print(river.count("ss"))
print(river.find("ss"))
print(river.find("x"))
```
wrong[2\n3\n-1]: Indexes start at 0: m=0, i=1, s=2. So the first "ss" starts at index 2.
wrong[2\n2\n0]: When the text isn't there, `find` gives -1, not 0. Index 0 would mean "found right at the start".
wrong[1\n2\n-1]: "ss" appears twice: mi(ss)i(ss)ippi. `count` counts every copy.
hint: `count` counts how many times the text appears. `find` gives the index of the first one.
hint: When the text isn't there at all, `find` gives -1.
right: "ss" appears twice in "mississippi", so `count` gives 2. The first "ss" starts at index 2 (m=0, i=1, s=2). And there's no "x", so `find` gives -1.
wrong: Write the indexes under the letters: m=0, i=1, s=2, s=3, i=4, s=5, s=6, and so on. "ss" starts at 2 and at 5, so `count` is 2 and `find` (the first one) is 2. "x" never appears, so `find` gives -1.

=== task: multi easy
title: Which ones are True?
prompt: Choose **every** expression that gives `True`.
* `"2026".isdigit()` :: Right. Every character is a digit.
- `"3.5".isdigit()` :: The point isn't a digit, so this is False. isdigit() needs **every** character to be a digit.
* `"report.pdf".endswith(".pdf")` :: Right. The last four characters are .pdf.
- `"Report".startswith("re")` :: Capital letters count: "Report" starts with "Re", not "re", so this is False.
- `"".isdigit()` :: The empty string has no characters at all, so isdigit() gives False.
hint: Two are correct.
hint: Check capital letters, and remember that isdigit() needs at least one character, and all of them digits.
right: "2026" is all digits, and "report.pdf" really ends with ".pdf". The others are False: "3.5" has a point, "Report" starts with a capital R, and the empty string has no digits at all.
wrong: Check each one exactly. isdigit() is True only when there's at least one character and every character is a digit. startswith and endswith compare characters exactly, capitals included.

=== task: parsons easy
title: Clean up a name
prompt: Arrange the lines into a program that trims the spaces from `raw`, gives the name a capital first letter, and prints:
```output
Grace
```
lines:
```python
raw = "  grace  "
name = raw.strip()
name = name.title()
print(name)
```
distractors:
```python
raw.strip()
name = raw.upper()
```
hint: A method's result has to be stored, or it's lost.
hint: Trim first, then fix the capital letter, then print.
right: `raw.strip()` makes a new string without the spaces, and `name = ...` keeps it. `name.title()` makes another new string with a capital G, and storing it back in `name` replaces the old value. A line like `raw.strip()` on its own would make the trimmed text and then throw it away.
wrong: Store every result: `name = raw.strip()`, then `name = name.title()`, then print. `raw.strip()` on its own doesn't change `raw`, and `.upper()` would make every letter a capital: GRACE.

=== task: predict medium
title: Find, replace, startswith
code:
```python
s = "the cat sat"
print(s.find("cat"))
print(s.find("dog"))
print(s.replace("at", "og"))
print(s.startswith("the"))
```
hint: `find` gives an index (counting from 0), or `-1` when the text isn't there.
hint: `replace` changes **every** match, not just the first.
right: `cat` starts at index 4 (`t-h-e-space` take 0 to 3). `dog` isn't there, so `find` gives `-1`. `replace` swaps both `at`s, giving `the cog sog`. And the string does start with `the`: `True`.
wrong: Line 2: count from 0, so `cat` starts at index 4. Line 3: a missing text gives `-1`. Line 4: `replace` changes every `at` → `og`. Line 5: `startswith` answers `True` or `False`.
wrong[5\n-1\nthe cog sog\nTrue]: Indexes start at 0: `t` is 0, `h` 1, `e` 2, space 3, so `c` is at index 4.
wrong[4\n0\nthe cog sog\nTrue]: When the text isn't found, `find` gives `-1`, not 0. Index 0 would mean "found at the very start".
wrong[4\n-1\nthe cog sat\nTrue]: `replace` changes **every** match. Both `cat` and `sat` contain `at`, so the result is `the cog sog`.

=== task: multi medium
title: Which ones make PYTHON?
prompt: Choose **every** expression whose value is exactly `"PYTHON"`.
* `"python".upper()` :: Right: every letter becomes a capital.
* `"Python".upper()` :: Right: letters that are already capitals stay capitals.
- `"python".title()` :: `title()` only capitalizes the first letter of each word: `"Python"`.
* `" python ".strip().upper()` :: Right: `strip()` removes the spaces, then `upper()` makes capitals.
- `"PYTHON".lower()` :: `lower()` goes the other way: `"python"`.
- `"python".upper` :: Without brackets the method never runs. This is the method itself, not the text `"PYTHON"`.
hint: Three of the six are right.
hint: Check each method name, and check for the round brackets that make a method run.
right: `upper()` turns every letter into a capital, whatever case it started in, and chaining `strip()` first removes the spaces. `title()` and `lower()` give other cases, and `upper` without brackets doesn't run at all.
wrong: Only `upper()` (with brackets) gives all capitals, and the spaces must be stripped first if there are any. `title()` gives `Python`, `lower()` gives `python`, and a method without `()` isn't called.

=== task: cells medium
title: Measure a messy string
prompt: `s` holds `"  Hello World  "` (2 spaces at each end). Work out each expression.
code:
```python
s = "  Hello World  "
```
labels: len(s) | len(s.strip()) | s.strip().find("o") | s.count("l") | s.strip().lower().find("w")
answer: 15 | 11 | 4 | 3 | 6
wrong[11 | 11 | 4 | 3 | 6]: `len(s)` counts every character, the 4 outer spaces too: 2 + 11 + 2 = 15.
wrong[15 | 11 | 6 | 3 | 6]: After `strip()` the string starts with `H` at index 0, so the first `o` (in Hello) is at index 4.
wrong[15 | 11 | 4 | 2 | 6]: `count("l")` counts every small `l`: two in `Hello` and one in `World`, so 3.
wrong[15 | 11 | 4 | 3 | -1]: `lower()` runs before `find`, so the W is already a small w, at index 6. `find` gives -1 only when the text isn't there at all.
