right: `find` gives the index of the `@`, wherever it is. `email[:at]` stops just before it, and `email[at + 1:]` starts just after it, so the `@` lands in neither piece. That's why the same code works for "x@y.z" and for "grace.hopper@navy.mil".
wrong: Find the `@` first: `at = email.find("@")`. Then `user = email[:at]` (everything before it) and `domain = email[at + 1:]` (everything after it).

